CHATGPT AND CIVIL 3D 2027 INTEGRATION CHANGE LOG
================================================
Date 2026-11-21
Project civil3d-mcp, bridge version 0.9.0

Scope
-----
This log records the ChatGPT integration changes, the rebuilt Windows package,
and the changes made to the local test session. Paths are relative to the
project folder.

01  README.md
-------------
Added
  - ChatGPT desktop setup using Settings > MCP servers and STDIO.
  - Codex TOML setup for an installed executable or a Python source checkout.
  - ChatGPT cloud setup using Secure MCP Tunnel and the server's --read-only
    option. It covers the tunnel client, workspace access, credentials, and
    checking the connection with a ping.
  - A description of the drawing data that can be returned to the MCP client.
Removed
  - The Claude-only setup as the primary installation path.
  - The claim that nothing leaves the computer.
Modified
  - Updated the architecture diagram, command examples, permission guidance,
    troubleshooting, update steps, and uninstall steps for ChatGPT and Codex.
  - Documented the separate roles of tool approvals and Civil 3D edit approval.
  - Kept a short Claude Desktop setup path using the existing JSON snippet.
Why
  - Users need instructions for the client they actually use. The bridge runs
    locally, and tool results can still go to the AI client.

02  package/install.bat
----------------------
Added
  - Generation of codex_mcp_config_snippet.toml alongside the installed server.
  - UTF-8 output and escaping for the full Windows executable path in the TOML.
  - A failure check for writing the TOML snippet.
  - Instructions for adding or updating civil3d in ChatGPT desktop using STDIO.
Removed
  - The Claude-only console instructions and automatic printing of Claude JSON.
Modified
  - Renamed the server install directory variable to C3D_INSTALL_DIR and quoted
    the directory assignments and printed executable path.
  - Continued writing the Claude JSON snippet using the renamed variable.
Why
  - The installer gives ChatGPT and Codex users a ready-to-use command and
    configuration, including when the installation path contains spaces.

03  package/INSTALL.txt
----------------------
Added
  - A reference to the browser and cloud setup in the README.
  - Instructions for updating an existing civil3d server entry.
Removed
  - Claude-only installation and permission directions as the default workflow.
Modified
  - Updated the quick-start steps for ChatGPT desktop, including opening a
    Civil 3D drawing, running MCPSTATUS, and verifying the connection.
  - Revised approval, update, and removal instructions for MCP clients.
Why
  - The quick guide should match the installer and the ChatGPT settings users
    see when they connect the tool.

04  package/uninstall.bat
------------------------
Added
  - Instructions to stop the tunnel runtime and remove the ChatGPT custom MCP
    server if a tunnel was configured.
Removed
  - The cleanup message that referred only to Claude Desktop.
Modified
  - The script now directs users to remove civil3d from ChatGPT desktop or the
    relevant client's configuration after running the local uninstall.
Why
  - Removing the local binaries and removing a client or tunnel connection are
    separate actions.

05  plugin/Core/WriteGate.cs
---------------------------
Added
  - Client-neutral wording in the Civil 3D permission prompt.
Removed
  - The wording that identified every edit requester as Claude.
Modified
  - The prompt title and body now identify the requester as an MCP client and
    include ChatGPT as an example.
Why
  - Users should be able to recognize the permission prompt when they send a
    request from ChatGPT or another MCP client.

06  plugin/Plugin.cs
-------------------
Added
  - Client-neutral command descriptions and status messages.
Removed
  - Claude-specific wording in the command comments and console output.
Modified
  - Described MCPSTATUS as selecting the Civil 3D instance for MCP calls.
  - Updated MCPALLOW and MCPREVOKE messages to describe MCP edit access.
Why
  - These commands apply to all clients connected to the Civil 3D bridge.

07  server/server.py
--------------------
Added
  - The --read-only command-line option. It registers only civil3d_read, even
    if C3D_MCP_SPLIT is enabled.
  - A server factory that registers tools for standard, split, and read-only
    operation.
  - A stdio runner that gives the MCP SDK separate stream buffers and restores
    the originals during shutdown.
Removed
  - The old module-level, two-mode tool registration block.
  - The SewerGEMS error instruction directing split-mode users to an edit tool
    that could only submit edit batches.
Modified
  - Read-only help excludes edit operations and the SewerGEMS import action.
  - Edit and import errors explain the required write-enabled tool.
  - The split edit tool now shares the standard write annotations, including
    idempotentHint=False to mark edits as unsafe to repeat automatically.
Why
  - Cloud users can connect with reads enabled and writes blocked by the server.
  - The stdio change fixes the packaged server's closed-stream error when the
    MCP client disconnects.

08  server/catalog.py
---------------------
Added
  - An include_edits option for generating full or read-only help.
Removed
  - The SewerGEMS write action from the read tool description and from help in
    read-only mode.
Modified
  - The catalogue renders the actions applicable to the selected mode. Standard
    help continues to include the full read and edit catalogue.
Why
  - Clients should discover the actions they can call in the selected mode.

09  tests/smoke_test.py
----------------------
Added
  - An end-to-end stdio regression test for --read-only with C3D_MCP_SPLIT=1.
  - Checks for the single read-only tool, its read-only annotation, ping,
    read-only help, and rejection of edits and SewerGEMS imports.
Removed
  - None.
Modified
  - Updated the test description and added the new test to the smoke suite.
Why
  - The test protects the read-only connection from accidentally exposing
    write tools while keeping the existing standard and split tests.

10  dist/C3dMcp-2027.zip
-----------------------
Added
  - ChatGPT desktop and cloud setup instructions inside the distribution.
  - The installer feature that generates the Codex TOML during installation.
Removed
  - Superseded server, add-in, installer, and documentation copies from the ZIP.
Modified
  - Rebuilt c3d-mcp-server.exe from the updated Python server.
  - Rebuilt bundle/C3dMcpBridge.bundle/Contents/2027/C3dMcpBridge.dll from the
    updated C# sources.
  - Refreshed the packaged README.md, INSTALL.txt, install.bat, and uninstall.bat.
  - Verified the ZIP contents against all seven build inputs, including the
    existing PackageContents.xml manifest.
Why
  - The installable release needs to contain the verified code and instructions.

11  Generated build output
--------------------------
Added
  - Temporary dist/_chatgpt_build staging files for PyInstaller and ZIP assembly.
Removed
  - The temporary staging folder after the final ZIP passed verification.
Modified
  - Regenerated the Release add-in and symbols under
    plugin/bin/Release/net10.0-windows and the associated .NET intermediates
